ck is Knight to d4 this is  the Blackburn shilling gambit again one  of those things where if you're not too  familiar with this opening from white  then I recommend not taking the gambit  here on e5 because that's kind of what  they want you to do here then their play  is Queen to g5 attacking both this  Knights here and the pawn here on g2  white could always take be pretty crazy  with Bishop taking here on f7 and then  the King to d8 now White's still going  to go down in material he's either gonna  lose this knight or he's going to lose  you know this pawn and potentially some  other material whether it's here on e4  or even some other pawns over here so I  wouldn't even get involved into this if  you see this unless you're super  familiar with the blackburn shilling  gamete instead I would just take this  night here on d4 force them to recapture  here and then you have options you can  always play castle on the King side you  could play my favorite which we've  already talked about before is the pawn  here to c3 seeing if they want to take  and you recapture with your night here  on c3 if they allow you to do this  you're almost never going to lose you  have all the material in the center of  the board your next moves usually going  to be pawn here to d4 they have really  nothing going on they've given up their  central pawn right here you have both  your central pawns they've given up  their knight which really kind of  counter-attack  the center of the board so you're doing  a great job right now and you haven't  even given up material on board which a  lot of times you'll do with that pawn  here to c3 just to gain that extra tempo  with your knight here so again if you  see the blackburn shilling i say just go  ahead and take the knight instead of  trying to take that material then here  on e5 so a lot of different variations  you can go into the italian game you can  decide if you want to be more aggressive  or more passive some of the variations  that we talked about whether these are  the the Evans gambit or the fried liver  attack or the Traxler gambit or even the  Blackburn Schilling gambit these are all  other videos I've created so you can  definitely watch those if you want some  more in-depth analysis on some of the  lines but Italian game definitely try it  out especially if you like to play pawn  here to e4 which I like to do it kind of  give you a change of pace from your  normal ride Lopez bring your Bishop here  to b5 or for me the King's gambit with  pawn here to f4 so hopefully you guys  enjoy this video hopefully you learned  something hopefully you can avoid some  of the traps in the Italian game and  I'll see you guys the next video thanks  for watching

